    "Model exhibits severe upward bias when recent history shows a spike (e.g., >83) followed by neutral sentiment; forecasts consistently overshoot true outcomes by 10-15%.",
    "True outcomes display aggressive mean reversion to the lower end of the historical range immediately after a price spike, whereas models predict a slow, linear recovery.",
    "Sentiment spikes (positive >0.20) in the history window correlate with model overestimation; negative sentiment (-0.33) does not sufficiently dampen the upward forecast bias.",
    "Forecast horizon error is systematic: models project 30-day paths that are significantly higher than actual realized paths, indicating a failure to capture short-term volatility and crash risk.",
    "When history ends near a local peak (e.g., 82-84), the model assumes continuation or slow decline, but true outcomes often drop sharply within the first 5 days of the forecast window."
